1
00:00:10,550 --> 00:00:14,832
Before any human being had ever held it in a bottle, we knew

2
00:00:14,832 --> 00:00:19,184
this element existed. Its first sample was not dug out of the

3
00:00:19,184 --> 00:00:23,677
ground. It was not boiled out of a rock, or squeezed out of the

4
00:00:23,677 --> 00:00:27,959
air. It arrived as a single bright yellow line, drawn in the

5
00:00:27,959 --> 00:00:32,521
light of the Sun, on a photographic plate exposed during a total

6
00:00:32,521 --> 00:00:36,593
eclipse. For twenty seven years, that line was the entire

7
00:00:36,593 --> 00:00:40,804
evidence. One line, and a name. The name was helium. And in

8
00:00:40,804 --> 00:00:45,227
1868, its only known address was one hundred and fifty million

9
00:00:45,227 --> 00:00:46,420
kilometres away.

10
00:00:48,320 --> 00:00:52,938
August the eighteenth, 1868. A total eclipse sweeps east across

11
00:00:52,938 --> 00:00:57,484
Asia. One of the men chasing it is Pierre Jules Cesar Janssen,

12
00:00:57,484 --> 00:01:01,668
forty four years old, a French astronomer who has already

13
00:01:01,668 --> 00:01:06,214
watched the Sun from Italy, and who has hauled his instruments

14
00:01:06,214 --> 00:01:10,832
to the edge of the Ganges plain at Guntur, in British India. He

15
00:01:10,832 --> 00:01:14,728
is not there to admire the corona. He is there with a

16
00:01:14,728 --> 00:01:19,202
spectroscope. A slit, a prism, and a spread of light into its

17
00:01:19,202 --> 00:01:23,603
colours. Nine years earlier, Gustav Kirchhoff had shown that

18
00:01:23,603 --> 00:01:28,149
every chemical element writes its own signature in a spectrum,

19
00:01:28,149 --> 00:01:32,406
bright lines at fixed wavelengths. So the question Janssen

20
00:01:32,406 --> 00:01:37,024
carries to India is simple. The flaming arcs at the Sun's edge,

21
00:01:37,024 --> 00:01:39,910
the prominences. What are they made of?

22
00:01:41,810 --> 00:01:46,415
Totality arrives. The dark Moon swallows the disc, and for a few

23
00:01:46,415 --> 00:01:50,736
minutes the prominences burn alone, released from the glare.

24
00:01:50,736 --> 00:01:54,703
Janssen sees their light split into sharp bright lines.

25
00:01:54,703 --> 00:01:59,166
Hydrogen. Sodium. The familiar signatures of things we already

26
00:01:59,166 --> 00:02:03,487
knew. And then a yellow line where no yellow line should be.

27
00:02:03,487 --> 00:02:08,021
Sodium writes two yellow lines, close together, labelled D1 and

28
00:02:08,021 --> 00:02:12,413
D2. This is a third one, a little to one side, at 587 point 6

29
00:02:12,413 --> 00:02:16,947
nanometres. It has no owner in the catalogue of known elements.

30
00:02:16,947 --> 00:02:21,410
And Janssen notices something better than the line itself. The

31
00:02:21,410 --> 00:02:25,802
prominences are so bright that he no longer needs the Moon to

32
00:02:25,802 --> 00:02:30,123
see them. He can measure the Sun's atmosphere any clear day,

33
00:02:30,123 --> 00:02:34,090
through a narrow slit, with the Sun blazing behind him.

34
00:02:35,990 --> 00:02:40,200
Same season, a different island. In London, Norman Lockyer,

35
00:02:40,200 --> 00:02:44,549
editor of Nature and a spectroscopist with a telescope in his

36
00:02:44,549 --> 00:02:48,969
garden, is running the same race. On the twentieth of October,

37
00:02:48,969 --> 00:02:53,319
1868, he records the same yellow line at the edge of the Sun.

38
00:02:53,319 --> 00:02:57,809
His letter and Janssen's cross in the post, and are read to the

39
00:02:57,809 --> 00:03:02,299
French Academy of Sciences on the same day, the twenty sixth of

40
00:03:02,299 --> 00:03:06,649
October. Rather than referee a quarrel, the Academy strikes a

41
00:03:06,649 --> 00:03:10,999
medal with both their faces on it. Then Lockyer goes one step

42
00:03:10,999 --> 00:03:15,559
further. With the chemist Edward Frankland he tries to reproduce

43
00:03:15,559 --> 00:03:19,909
the line in the laboratory. Hydrogen under pressure. Hydrogen

44
00:03:19,909 --> 00:03:24,259
under heat. Nothing. And if no substance on Earth writes that

45
00:03:24,259 --> 00:03:28,468
line, then the line belongs to a substance not yet found on

46
00:03:28,468 --> 00:03:32,959
Earth. They name it after Helios, the Greek Sun. Helium. Fitted

47
00:03:32,959 --> 00:03:37,519
with the ium of a metal, because everyone assumed it had to be a

48
00:03:37,519 --> 00:03:38,010
metal.

49
00:03:39,910 --> 00:03:44,423
Not everyone is convinced. A new element announced in the sky,

50
00:03:44,423 --> 00:03:48,864
with no sample, no weight, no density, is a large claim. Some

51
00:03:48,864 --> 00:03:53,233
chemists argue the line is an effect of pressure, or a third

52
00:03:53,233 --> 00:03:57,889
sodium line that nobody had yet noticed on Earth. And the record

53
00:03:57,889 --> 00:04:02,545
needs correcting here, because the tidy version of this story is

54
00:04:02,545 --> 00:04:07,201
wrong. Janssen did not name helium, and in 1868 he did not claim

55
00:04:07,201 --> 00:04:11,785
a new element. He found the line. Lockyer and Frankland read it

56
00:04:11,785 --> 00:04:16,226
as a new one. Two years later, Janssen was trapped inside the

57
00:04:16,226 --> 00:04:20,739
siege of Paris, and to reach an eclipse in Algeria he left the

58
00:04:20,739 --> 00:04:25,109
city by balloon, on the second of December, 1870. He reached

59
00:04:25,109 --> 00:04:29,693
Oran. Cloud covered the Sun. That is the trade: ground truth is

60
00:04:29,693 --> 00:04:32,200
occasionally delivered by balloon.

61
00:04:34,100 --> 00:04:38,383
Meanwhile, the element had been sitting on a laboratory shelf.

62
00:04:38,383 --> 00:04:42,190
In 1888 and again in 1890, the American chemist William

63
00:04:42,190 --> 00:04:46,473
Hillebrand treated the uranium mineral uraninite with acid and

64
00:04:46,473 --> 00:04:50,756
watched an inert gas bubble off. He tested it. It did nothing.

65
00:04:50,756 --> 00:04:55,039
He concluded it was nitrogen, and he published a careful paper

66
00:04:55,039 --> 00:04:59,458
about it. He was one element away. In London, William Ramsay had

67
00:04:59,458 --> 00:05:03,741
just isolated argon from air with Lord Rayleigh. An unreactive

68
00:05:03,741 --> 00:05:08,160
gas that fit no column of Mendeleev's table. Hunting for more of

69
00:05:08,160 --> 00:05:12,035
the same, Ramsay read Hillebrand's paper and doubted the

70
00:05:12,035 --> 00:05:16,386
nitrogen. He repeated the experiment on cleveite, a uranium ore

71
00:05:16,386 --> 00:05:20,737
from Norway, on the twenty sixth of March, 1895, and he put the

72
00:05:20,737 --> 00:05:25,020
gas into a glass tube and looked at it through a spectroscope.

73
00:05:26,920 --> 00:05:31,625
There it was. The same yellow, at 587 point 6 nanometres, in a

74
00:05:31,625 --> 00:05:36,405
tube in a London laboratory. Ramsay sent samples to Lockyer and

75
00:05:36,405 --> 00:05:41,110
to William Crookes, whose measurements could be trusted to the

76
00:05:41,110 --> 00:05:45,218
last decimal place. Crookes confirmed the identity and

77
00:05:45,218 --> 00:05:49,624
telegraphed Paris. The element in the Sun was in a rock in

78
00:05:49,624 --> 00:05:53,956
England. In Sweden, Per Teodor Cleve and his student Nils

79
00:05:53,956 --> 00:05:58,511
Langlet pulled helium out of the same ore independently, and

80
00:05:58,511 --> 00:06:03,291
measured its atomic weight at close to four. The periodic table

81
00:06:03,291 --> 00:06:08,146
now had two orphans: argon, which fit nowhere, and helium, which

82
00:06:08,146 --> 00:06:12,776
had just turned up where it was supposed to be impossible. In

83
00:06:12,776 --> 00:06:17,257
1902 Mendeleev gave the inert gases their own column, group

84
00:06:17,257 --> 00:06:22,112
zero, and stopped insisting that this was a mistake. The Sun had

85
00:06:22,112 --> 00:06:23,680
corrected the table.

86
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:29,824
But where had it come from? Not from the rock. The rock was only

87
00:06:29,824 --> 00:06:33,742
a container. Uranium and thorium in the ore decay through a

88
00:06:33,742 --> 00:06:37,855
chain of unstable nuclei, and one of the things they throw off

89
00:06:37,855 --> 00:06:41,904
is an alpha particle. Two protons and two neutrons. That is a

90
00:06:41,904 --> 00:06:45,952
helium nucleus. It picks up two electrons, and it stops being

91
00:06:45,952 --> 00:06:50,196
dangerous and starts being a gas. Trapped in crystal, it can sit

92
00:06:50,196 --> 00:06:54,179
there for hundreds of millions of years. Which is why helium

93
00:06:54,179 --> 00:06:58,032
comes out of the same wells as natural gas. Both have been

94
00:06:58,032 --> 00:07:02,145
seeping out of ancient granite into the same porous traps, for

95
00:07:02,145 --> 00:07:06,193
longer than the rock has been rock. The element that lived in

96
00:07:06,193 --> 00:07:10,372
the Sun had been under Texas and Kansas the whole time, leaking

97
00:07:10,372 --> 00:07:10,960
quietly.

98
00:07:12,860 --> 00:07:17,151
Which changes what you can build. Helium is the second lightest

99
00:07:17,151 --> 00:07:21,308
gas, and it is inert. It will not burn, and it will not react

100
00:07:21,308 --> 00:07:25,598
with anything it touches. During the First World War the United

101
00:07:25,598 --> 00:07:29,487
States began extracting it seriously, and by the nineteen

102
00:07:29,487 --> 00:07:33,577
twenties airships were filling with it. Which is exactly why

103
00:07:33,577 --> 00:07:37,733
1937 looks the way it does. The Hindenburg, seven hundred and

104
00:07:37,733 --> 00:07:42,024
sixty feet of hydrogen, burned at Lakehurst, while the American

105
00:07:42,024 --> 00:07:46,181
ships flying the same routes were forbidden to sell helium to

106
00:07:46,181 --> 00:07:50,204
Germany. But lifting is the easy story. Helium's refusal to

107
00:07:50,204 --> 00:07:54,494
react is the whole point. A shield gas for welding titanium and

108
00:07:54,494 --> 00:07:58,852
aluminium. A purge gas for engines that cannot tolerate a single

109
00:07:58,852 --> 00:08:02,942
stray spark. The gas you fill a leak detector with, when one

110
00:08:02,942 --> 00:08:04,350
atom a year matters.

111
00:08:06,250 --> 00:08:10,600
And then, in 1908, Heike Kamerlingh Onnes liquefied it, at about

112
00:08:10,600 --> 00:08:14,817
four degrees above absolute zero. Nothing else in nature stays

113
00:08:14,817 --> 00:08:19,033
liquid below five kelvin. That one property went on to rebuild

114
00:08:19,033 --> 00:08:23,183
physics and medicine. Liquid helium cools the superconducting

115
00:08:23,183 --> 00:08:27,533
magnets inside a hospital MRI scanner, in the detectors at CERN,

116
00:08:27,533 --> 00:08:31,683
and in the magnets that steer protons around the Large Hadron

117
00:08:31,683 --> 00:08:36,033
Collider. It holds samples at temperatures where chemistry stops

118
00:08:36,033 --> 00:08:39,982
moving. It kept the instruments of the space age cold in a

119
00:08:39,982 --> 00:08:43,797
vacuum that was trying to heat them. And when we finally

120
00:08:43,797 --> 00:08:48,081
measured the universe honestly, helium came out at about twenty

121
00:08:48,081 --> 00:08:52,364
four percent of the mass of all ordinary matter. Second only to

122
00:08:52,364 --> 00:08:56,581
hydrogen. Almost all of it made in the first minutes after the

123
00:08:56,581 --> 00:08:57,250
Big Bang.
